1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:06,860
The brush reveals a leaf with lines of writing. But the words on it are far older than the leaf.

2
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:13,614
They were spoken and remembered for many centuries before anyone wrote them down. This is a hymn from the Rigveda.

3
00:00:13,614 --> 00:00:16,790
Who spoke these words, and where?

4
00:00:32,283 --> 00:00:38,229
How do we know? Here the evidence is not mainly from the ground. It is a text.

5
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:43,161
The Rigveda is a collection of hymns, learnt by heart and passed down by voice.

6
00:00:43,161 --> 00:00:47,482
Archaeology cannot yet match it to one clear set of sites.

7
00:01:01,483 --> 00:01:05,389
The Rigveda has one thousand and twenty-eight hymns, in ten books.

8
00:01:05,389 --> 00:01:12,975
It describes the region of the Sapta Sindhu, the land of seven rivers, in the north-west. The people were mainly pastoral.

9
00:01:12,975 --> 00:01:20,578
Cattle were their wealth. Even the word for war meant a search for cows. They also valued the horse and the chariot.

10
00:01:20,578 --> 00:01:28,668
They lived in tribes, called jana. A tribe was led by a chief, the rajan. His priest was the purohita.

11
00:01:28,668 --> 00:01:34,289
The clan was the kula, and the village was the grama. Power was shared with assemblies.

12
00:01:34,289 --> 00:01:37,469
The sabha and the samiti advised the chief.

13
00:01:37,469 --> 00:01:43,408
Women could attend, and women such as Lopamudra and Ghosha are named as composers of hymns.

14
00:01:43,408 --> 00:01:49,657
The best-known battle in the Rigveda is the Battle of the Ten Kings, fought on the river Parushni, which is the Ravi.

15
00:01:49,657 --> 00:01:56,799
Sudas of the Bharata tribe won. Society was not yet rigid. Farming was secondary to herding.

16
00:01:56,799 --> 00:02:00,865
Gold was used as an ornament, and trade was by barter.

17
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:19,776
Scholars disagree. Who were the Vedic people, and where did they come from?

18
00:02:19,776 --> 00:02:28,655
One view says that Indo-Aryan speakers migrated into north-western India from Central Asia over time, and cites language and recent genetic studies.

19
00:02:28,655 --> 00:02:33,001
Another view says that the Vedic culture grew up within India itself.

20
00:02:33,001 --> 00:02:37,955
This is a long and sensitive debate, and the evidence is still being studied.

21
00:02:51,950 --> 00:03:00,153
Carve this tip into memory. Rivers, cows, rajan, sabha:. a herding world with shared power.

22
00:03:00,153 --> 00:03:06,808
Four words give you the Early Vedic world: rivers, cows, a chief and an assembly.

23
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:37,253
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name the oldest Veda, a collection of hymns. The answer: the Rigveda. Number two.

24
00:03:37,253 --> 00:03:48,677
What was the tribal chief called in the Early Vedic age? The answer: the rajan. Number three.

25
00:03:48,677 --> 00:03:59,807
What was the main form of wealth in the Rigveda? The answer: cattle. Now one line for Mains.

26
00:03:59,807 --> 00:04:04,544
Describe the political and social life of the Early Vedic period.

27
00:04:22,517 --> 00:04:30,630
The manuscript leaf goes into its case. The words on it were first spoken, not written. Three facts to take away.

28
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:40,155
Hymns of the Rigveda, learnt by heart. Dated to about 1500 to 1000 BCE. Cattle, chiefs and open assemblies.

29
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:43,854
Next gallery: The Later Vedic Age.

